WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.720
The Olympic torch is about to be extinguished in a blaze of glory by the U.S. track team.

00:07.720 --> 00:12.680
In the 400-meter relay, the American Quartet is off to a flying start as Paul Drayton runs

00:12.680 --> 00:13.680
the first leg.

00:13.680 --> 00:18.160
The U.S. holds both the present Olympic and world records in this event, but both marks

00:18.160 --> 00:20.240
go by the board today.

00:20.240 --> 00:24.720
It's Jerry Ashworth running the second leg, and he passes the baton to Dick Stebbins,

00:24.720 --> 00:34.720
who is off on the third 100-meter lap.

00:34.720 --> 00:38.480
Then Stebbins passes to Bob Hayes, and watch that man take off.

00:38.480 --> 00:43.280
Hayes, left, literally flies down the track as he heads for the wire to make a 39-second

00:43.280 --> 00:51.760
splat for the U.S.

00:51.760 --> 00:58.520
Hayes and company contribute brilliantly to the U.S. gold medal total of 36.

00:58.520 --> 01:04.200
Then the start of the marathon run, 26 miles and 385 yards.

01:04.200 --> 01:10.800
68 runners start in this crime, but in the marathon, many are called, but few are chosen.

01:10.800 --> 01:15.440
As they leave the stadium for the run through the streets of Tokyo, it is estimated that

01:15.440 --> 01:18.080
more than a million and a half people line the route.

01:18.080 --> 01:22.800
Favored in the race is the winner of the 1960 Olympic run, a member of Haile Selassie's

01:22.800 --> 01:33.560
Ethiopian Palace Guard, Avabii Bekela.

01:33.560 --> 01:37.600
This is Bekela running with the pack at the 10-mile mark, just taking it easy.

01:37.600 --> 01:44.200
This year, he has socks and sneakers on, but he ran barefooted in the 1960 Olympics.

01:44.200 --> 01:50.360
Bekela takes the lead just before the halfway mark, and he's never headed all the way home.

01:50.360 --> 01:54.520
He leaves the field far behind as he nears the finish, and he approaches the stadium

01:54.520 --> 01:57.080
a full four minutes ahead of the second man.

01:57.080 --> 02:02.960
Now it's once around the track, two rousing cheers before he bursts the tape.

02:02.960 --> 02:10.040
Bekela chops four minutes and five seconds off his winning time of four years ago as

02:10.040 --> 02:13.040
he crosses the finish still running effortlessly.

02:13.040 --> 02:21.200
He has enough energy left to simmer down with calisthenics, gets a popular victory.

02:21.200 --> 02:25.120
The 18th Modern Olympics is then brought to a close in moving ceremonies that ring down

02:25.120 --> 02:27.320
the curtain in Olympic Stadium.

02:27.320 --> 02:32.640
In contrast to the formal entry on opening day, the teams mingle together in an atmosphere

02:32.640 --> 02:35.020
of exuberance and regret.

02:35.020 --> 02:43.640
But until 1968, will there be another meeting like this one?

02:43.640 --> 02:54.920
Then the Olympic torch is extinguished, not to brighten the horizons of international

02:54.920 --> 02:59.520
sportsmanship until it is relit in Mexico City.

02:59.520 --> 03:04.140
An honor guard carries the Olympic banner from the stadium as 200 torches make a jeweled

03:04.140 --> 03:11.760
circle of friendship as the Japanese word for farewell goes up on the scoreboard.

03:11.760 --> 03:19.560
So it's sayonara to the first Olympic Games to be held in the Far East.

03:19.560 --> 03:34.640
Hasta la vista in Mexico.

